§§ `Article 13(13) — the fideicommis carve-out`

-- A court-approved fideicommis, registered as a charity, whose constitution DOES purport to
-- permit the addition of a non-charitable purpose — but where paragraphs (2) and (3) are
-- inconsistent with an order of the court under the Loi of 1862. The carve-out disapplies
-- (2) and (3): the nullity rule does NOT bite, and the amendment is not subject to the
-- Commissioner's evidence-and-approval proviso. The 1862 order prevails.
`the Le Gallais fideicommis` MEANS RegisteredCharity WITH
    name                                                            IS "the Le Gallais fideicommis"
    `the charity as tested under Article 5`                         IS `Victoria College Trust`
    `the entity is registered as a charity, and has not been deregistered`  IS TRUE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to permit an amendment of the purposes of the entity to include a purpose that is not charitable`  IS TRUE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to permit an amendment of the purposes of the entity to add or substitute a different charitable purpose`  IS TRUE
    `the entity has provided evidence, to the satisfaction of the Commissioner, of a reason justifying the proposed amendment`  IS FALSE   -- no evidence given...
    `the entity has obtained the Commissioner's prior approval of the proposed amendment`  IS FALSE   -- ...and no approval sought
    `the entity provides public benefit in accordance with its registered public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the entity requests the Commissioner's approval of a proposed amendment to that statement`  IS FALSE
    `the entity provides evidence, to the satisfaction of the Commissioner, of a reason justifying that request`  IS FALSE
    `the charity is entered in the restricted section of the register`  IS FALSE
    `it meets the condition, prescribed under Article 9(2), as to which the Commissioner was satisfied under Article 9(6)(b)`  IS FALSE
    `before it ceased to meet that condition, the Commissioner gave prior permission for it to remain entered in the restricted section on the basis of a different condition prescribed under that Article`  IS FALSE
    `it meets that different condition as specified in that permission`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(a) a change in a matter about which information is entered on the register (whether in the general or restricted section) in relation to that charity`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(b) any other matter, coming to light after registration or after permission for a change of name, that could, if known before the registration or permission, have led to the Commissioner refusing to register the charity, refusing to accept the name of the charity, or entering it in a different section`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(c) a proposal to change the charity's constitution`         IS FALSE
    `(6)(d) a proposal that could result, or an event that has resulted, in the charity ceasing to meet the requirements of Article 11(4)(c) or (d)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(e) a reportable matter, in relation to a governor of the charity, of which the charity is aware that the governor has not promptly reported it to the Commissioner under Article 19(2)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(f) a matter likely to lead to the charity becoming bankrupt, being wound up or otherwise ceasing to exist`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(g) if the charity is entered in the restricted section, a proposal to change how or whether the charity meets a condition prescribed under Article 9(2)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(h) any other matter prescribed by the Minister by Order for this purpose`  IS FALSE
    `a statement of current intentions as to future payments has been provided under Article 13(9)(b)`  IS FALSE
    `the charity has made a payment that is inconsistent with that statement`  IS FALSE
    `the entity is a court-approved fideicommis or an incorporated 1862 association`  IS TRUE
    `paragraphs (2) and (3) are inconsistent with an order made, whether before or after registration under this Law, by the court under the Loi of 1862`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT `Article 13(13) — the fideicommis or 1862-association carve-out applies` `the Le Gallais fideicommis`
-- (2) does NOT bite: the constitutional provision is NOT of no effect.
#ASSERT NOT `Article 13(2) — the purported power to add a non-charitable purpose is of no effect` `the Le Gallais fideicommis`
-- (3) does NOT bite: the amendment may be made WITHOUT the Commissioner's evidence and
-- prior approval, because the 1862 order says so.
#ASSERT `Article 13(3) — the proposed amendment of purposes may be made` `the Le Gallais fideicommis`

§§ `Article 13 — the duties, exercised`

-- The charity in good standing provides public benefit within the year: FULFILLED.
#TRACE `Article 13(4) — the duty to provide public benefit` `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `provide public benefit in accordance with the registered public benefit statement` AT 200

-- Art 13(6): a reportable matter has arisen (a proposal to change the constitution). The
-- charity reports on day 5, inside the "promptly" placeholder of 14 days: FULFILLED.
#TRACE `Article 13(6) — the duty to report promptly` `Jersey Heritage Trust, misbehaving` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `report the matter to the Commissioner` AT 5

-- The SAME charity, which never reports. A MUST: the deadline passes and it is a BREACH.
#TRACE `Article 13(6) — the duty to report promptly` `Jersey Heritage Trust, misbehaving` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `send an annual return` AT 400

-- Art 13(7): the annual return, sent.
#TRACE `Article 13(7) — the duty to send an annual return` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 350

-- Art 13(11): the charity makes the inconsistent payment. SHANT — doing the act IS the breach.
#TRACE `Article 13(11) — payments inconsistent with the governor-payment statement` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `make a payment that is inconsistent with a statement provided under Article 13(9)(b)` AT 100

-- Art 13(11): the LAWFUL route — the charity applies for permission to amend the statement
-- instead, and makes no inconsistent payment. The SHANT is honoured; the MAY is exercised.
#TRACE `Article 13(11) — payments inconsistent with the governor-payment statement` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `apply to the Commissioner, with reasons, for permission to amend that statement` AT 100

§§ `Article 14 — the court's powers`

-- An order of a KIND EXPRESSLY LISTED: suspending a governor, with ancillary provision.
`an order suspending a governor, with ancillary provision` MEANS Part4CourtOrder WITH
    description                                                     IS "suspending Ms Le Brun, with ancillary directions"
    `(a)(i) prohibiting the charity or a governor from referring to or holding out the entity as a charity or Jersey charity, or as being charitable, having charitable purposes or providing public benefit`  IS FALSE
    `(a)(ii) prohibiting the charity or a governor from soliciting donations from the public, as specified in the order`  IS FALSE
    `(a)(iii) prohibiting the charity or a governor from taking any other action specified in the order`  IS FALSE
    `(b) appointing a person (whether for a specified or indefinite period) to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors`  IS FALSE
    `(c) appointing a person to be a governor of the charity`        IS FALSE
    `(d) suspending or removing a governor of the charity, or any other person concerned in the management or control of the charity`  IS TRUE
    `(e) requiring a person (including without prejudice a person carrying on regulated financial services business) who holds property on behalf of the charity, or on behalf of a governor of the charity, not to part with the property without the court's consent`  IS FALSE
    `(f) restricting the transactions that may be entered into, or the nature or amount of the payments that may be made, in the administration of the charity without the court's consent`  IS FALSE
    `(g) making the administration of the charity subject to such supervision, restraint or conditions, from such time, and for such periods, as the court may specify`  IS FALSE
    `(h) requiring a governor who appears to the court to have engaged in any misconduct, or any other person who appears to be responsible for any misconduct, to take such steps as the court may direct to remedy the effects of the misconduct`  IS FALSE
    `(i) making such ancillary provision as the court thinks desirable`  IS TRUE
    `is an order of some other kind, not among those listed in paragraph (3)`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 14(3) — the order is among those expressly listed` `an order suspending a governor, with ancillary provision`

-- An order of a kind NOWHERE in the list. It is NOT within paragraph (3) — and it does not
-- need to be: paragraph (2) is the general power, and (3) is "without prejudice to the
-- generality" of it. The model shows the list is non-exhaustive rather than merely saying so.
`an order the court sees fit, of a kind not listed` MEANS Part4CourtOrder WITH
    description                                                     IS "directing a forensic audit at the charity's expense"
    `(a)(i) prohibiting the charity or a governor from referring to or holding out the entity as a charity or Jersey charity, or as being charitable, having charitable purposes or providing public benefit`  IS FALSE
    `(a)(ii) prohibiting the charity or a governor from soliciting donations from the public, as specified in the order`  IS FALSE
    `(a)(iii) prohibiting the charity or a governor from taking any other action specified in the order`  IS FALSE
    `(b) appointing a person (whether for a specified or indefinite period) to manage the affairs of the charity in place of its governors`  IS FALSE
    `(c) appointing a person to be a governor of the charity`        IS FALSE
    `(d) suspending or removing a governor of the charity, or any other person concerned in the management or control of the charity`  IS FALSE
    `(e) requiring a person (including without prejudice a person carrying on regulated financial services business) who holds property on behalf of the charity, or on behalf of a governor of the charity, not to part with the property without the court's consent`  IS FALSE
    `(f) restricting the transactions that may be entered into, or the nature or amount of the payments that may be made, in the administration of the charity without the court's consent`  IS FALSE
    `(g) making the administration of the charity subject to such supervision, restraint or conditions, from such time, and for such periods, as the court may specify`  IS FALSE
    `(h) requiring a governor who appears to the court to have engaged in any misconduct, or any other person who appears to be responsible for any misconduct, to take such steps as the court may direct to remedy the effects of the misconduct`  IS FALSE
    `(i) making such ancillary provision as the court thinks desirable`  IS FALSE
    `is an order of some other kind, not among those listed in paragraph (3)`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 14(3) — the order is among those expressly listed` `an order the court sees fit, of a kind not listed`

-- GATE (a): the Commissioner applies, alleging misconduct. Part 1's `is misconduct` decides
-- the allegation; we reuse its `breach of a required steps notice by the charity`.
`the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct` MEANS Article14Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, misbehaving`
    `the application is made by the Commissioner or the Attorney General`  IS TRUE
    `the conduct alleged`                                           IS `breach of a required steps notice by the charity`
    `it appears to the court that the conduct alleged is or has been in the administration of the charity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the property of the charity or securing a proper application of that property in accordance with the charity's registered charitable purposes and registered public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the application to amend or revoke is made by the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or a person to whom the order was directed`  IS TRUE
    `the constitution of the charity purports to restrict or exclude the powers of the court`  IS TRUE   -- 14(5)(b): irrelevant, and shown to be

#ASSERT `Article 14(1)(a) — misconduct in the administration of the charity` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 14(1)(b) — necessary or desirable to protect the property` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct`
-- Gate (a) ALONE opens the power: the gates are ALTERNATIVE.
#ASSERT `Article 14(1) — the court's power arises` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct`
-- ART 14(5)(b), OPERATIVE: the constitution purports to exclude the court's powers, and the
-- power arises anyway. "Despite anything in the constitution of the charity."

-- GATE (b) ALONE: no misconduct at all, but the property needs protecting.
`the Attorney General's Article 14 application, to protect the property` MEANS Article14Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the application is made by the Commissioner or the Attorney General`  IS TRUE
    `the conduct alleged`                                           IS `money laundering by an outsider, in relation to the charity`
    `it appears to the court that the conduct alleged is or has been in the administration of the charity`  IS FALSE   -- the outsider is not in the administration
    `(1)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the property of the charity or securing a proper application of that property in accordance with the charity's registered charitable purposes and registered public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the application to amend or revoke is made by the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or a person to whom the order was directed`  IS TRUE
    `the constitution of the charity purports to restrict or exclude the powers of the court`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 14(1)(a) — misconduct in the administration of the charity` `the Attorney General's Article 14 application, to protect the property`
#ASSERT `Article 14(1)(b) — necessary or desirable to protect the property` `the Attorney General's Article 14 application, to protect the property`
#ASSERT `Article 14(1) — the court's power arises` `the Attorney General's Article 14 application, to protect the property`

-- NO STANDING: a disgruntled donor applies. Misconduct or not, the court's Article 14 power
-- does not arise: the chapeau is a CONJUNCT.
`a donor's Article 14 application (no standing)` MEANS Article14Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, misbehaving`
    `the application is made by the Commissioner or the Attorney General`  IS FALSE   -- the only change that matters
    `the conduct alleged`                                           IS `breach of a required steps notice by the charity`
    `it appears to the court that the conduct alleged is or has been in the administration of the charity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the property of the charity or securing a proper application of that property in accordance with the charity's registered charitable purposes and registered public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the application to amend or revoke is made by the Commissioner, the Attorney General, the registered charity or a person to whom the order was directed`  IS FALSE
    `the constitution of the charity purports to restrict or exclude the powers of the court`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 14(1)(a) — misconduct in the administration of the charity` `a donor's Article 14 application (no standing)`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 14(1) — the court's power arises` `a donor's Article 14 application (no standing)`

-- The court exercises the Article 14 power: it makes an order.
#TRACE `Article 14(2) — the court's power to make any order it sees fit` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Court DOES `make an order in respect of the charity` AT 60

-- The court DECLINES to make any order. Article 14(2) is a MAY: benign omission is
-- FULFILLED, not BREACH. A court that hears the application and does nothing has not
-- breached the Law.
#TRACE `Article 14(2) — the court's power to make any order it sees fit` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `report the matter to the Commissioner` AT 200

-- Art 14(4): the charity applies to have the order revoked, and the court revokes it.
#TRACE `Article 14(4) — the court's power to amend or revoke an order` `the Commissioner's Article 14 application, alleging misconduct` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Court DOES `amend or revoke an order made under Article 14` AT 80

§§ `Article 15 — deregistration on application by the entity`

-- The entity applies, complies with the information request, and there are no grounds
-- relating to its proposals for refusing. The Commissioner MUST deregister — the ROR
-- collapses to a single duty, because limb (b) is not available.
`Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (clean)` MEANS Article15Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the entity has provided the Commissioner with any information or document requested by the Commissioner in relation to the entity's proposals for the continuation or winding up of the entity and for the application of any of its property remaining after deregistration`  IS TRUE
    `there are grounds, relating to the proposals mentioned in paragraph (2), for refusing to deregister the entity`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 15(2) — the entity has complied with paragraph (2)` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (clean)`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 15(3)(b) — refusal is available` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (clean)`

-- The entity complies, but its proposals for the application of the remaining property are
-- unsatisfactory. NOW limb (b) is open, and the Commissioner has a genuine choice.
`Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (bad proposals)` MEANS Article15Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the entity has provided the Commissioner with any information or document requested by the Commissioner in relation to the entity's proposals for the continuation or winding up of the entity and for the application of any of its property remaining after deregistration`  IS TRUE
    `there are grounds, relating to the proposals mentioned in paragraph (2), for refusing to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT `Article 15(3)(b) — refusal is available` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (bad proposals)`

-- The entity does NOT comply with the information request. Article 15(3) never engages:
-- "IF the entity complies with paragraph (2) the Commissioner must -". No duty arises.
`Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (non-compliant)` MEANS Article15Application WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the entity has provided the Commissioner with any information or document requested by the Commissioner in relation to the entity's proposals for the continuation or winding up of the entity and for the application of any of its property remaining after deregistration`  IS FALSE
    `there are grounds, relating to the proposals mentioned in paragraph (2), for refusing to deregister the entity`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 15(2) — the entity has complied with paragraph (2)` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (non-compliant)`

-- The entity applies and then supplies the requested information: the MAY's HENCE carries
-- the duty, and the duty is discharged.
#TRACE `Articles 15(1) and 15(2) — the entity's application and its duty to inform` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Applicant DOES `apply to the Commissioner to terminate the registration of the entity` AT 10
    PARTY Applicant DOES `provide the information or document requested in relation to the proposals` AT 20

-- The entity applies and then goes silent. The Art 15(2) duty is BREACHED.
#TRACE `Articles 15(1) and 15(2) — the entity's application and its duty to inform` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Applicant DOES `apply to the Commissioner to terminate the registration of the entity` AT 10

-- Art 15(3), clean application: the Commissioner deregisters. FULFILLED.
#TRACE `Article 15(3) — the Commissioner must deregister or refuse` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (clean)` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `deregister the entity` AT 30

-- Art 15(3), clean application, and the Commissioner does NOTHING. This is a MUST, not a
-- MAY: sitting on the file is a BREACH. Contrast the Article 14 and Article 16(1) traces.
#TRACE `Article 15(3) — the Commissioner must deregister or refuse` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (clean)` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 200

-- Art 15(3), bad proposals: the Commissioner refuses, on grounds relating to the proposals.
-- The ROR is satisfied by EITHER arm.
#TRACE `Article 15(3) — the Commissioner must deregister or refuse` `Jersey Heritage Trust's deregistration application (bad proposals)` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `refuse to deregister the entity` AT 30

§ `Article 16 — THE CENTRAL TEST SUITE: the MAY and the MUST`

§§ `The four grounds, made out in turn`

-- GROUND (a): the entity no longer meets the charity test. We give it the Part 3
-- `the Jersey Arts Quango` — a CandidateCharity that FAILS the test (its constitution lets
-- the Minister direct it, so the Art 5(2) knock-out bites).
`the Quango, no longer meeting the charity test` MEANS RegisteredCharity WITH
    name                                                            IS "the Jersey Arts Quango"
    `the charity as tested under Article 5`                         IS `the Jersey Arts Quango`
    `the entity is registered as a charity, and has not been deregistered`  IS TRUE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to permit an amendment of the purposes of the entity to include a purpose that is not charitable`  IS FALSE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to permit an amendment of the purposes of the entity to add or substitute a different charitable purpose`  IS FALSE
    `the entity has provided evidence, to the satisfaction of the Commissioner, of a reason justifying the proposed amendment`  IS FALSE
    `the entity has obtained the Commissioner's prior approval of the proposed amendment`  IS FALSE
    `the entity provides public benefit in accordance with its registered public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the entity requests the Commissioner's approval of a proposed amendment to that statement`  IS FALSE
    `the entity provides evidence, to the satisfaction of the Commissioner, of a reason justifying that request`  IS FALSE
    `the charity is entered in the restricted section of the register`  IS FALSE
    `it meets the condition, prescribed under Article 9(2), as to which the Commissioner was satisfied under Article 9(6)(b)`  IS FALSE
    `before it ceased to meet that condition, the Commissioner gave prior permission for it to remain entered in the restricted section on the basis of a different condition prescribed under that Article`  IS FALSE
    `it meets that different condition as specified in that permission`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(a) a change in a matter about which information is entered on the register (whether in the general or restricted section) in relation to that charity`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(b) any other matter, coming to light after registration or after permission for a change of name, that could, if known before the registration or permission, have led to the Commissioner refusing to register the charity, refusing to accept the name of the charity, or entering it in a different section`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(c) a proposal to change the charity's constitution`         IS FALSE
    `(6)(d) a proposal that could result, or an event that has resulted, in the charity ceasing to meet the requirements of Article 11(4)(c) or (d)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(e) a reportable matter, in relation to a governor of the charity, of which the charity is aware that the governor has not promptly reported it to the Commissioner under Article 19(2)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(f) a matter likely to lead to the charity becoming bankrupt, being wound up or otherwise ceasing to exist`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(g) if the charity is entered in the restricted section, a proposal to change how or whether the charity meets a condition prescribed under Article 9(2)`  IS FALSE
    `(6)(h) any other matter prescribed by the Minister by Order for this purpose`  IS FALSE
    `a statement of current intentions as to future payments has been provided under Article 13(9)(b)`  IS FALSE
    `the charity has made a payment that is inconsistent with that statement`  IS FALSE
    `the entity is a court-approved fideicommis or an incorporated 1862 association`  IS FALSE
    `paragraphs (2) and (3) are inconsistent with an order made, whether before or after registration under this Law, by the court under the Loi of 1862`  IS FALSE

-- GROUND (a), everything else in order: notice given, objections invited, proportionate.
`Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `the Quango, no longer meeting the charity test`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 16(1)(a) — the entity no longer meets the charity test` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the procedural precondition (notice and invitation to object)` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the proportionality requirement` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(2) — the duty to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met`

-- GROUND (b): the Commissioner was misled. The charity STILL meets the charity test — the
-- Quango's problem is not this charity's problem — so ground (a) is NOT made out, and (b)
-- carries the case alone.
`Article 16 case — ground (b), the Commissioner was misled` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1)(a) — the entity no longer meets the charity test` `Article 16 case — ground (b), the Commissioner was misled`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1)(b) — the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity` `Article 16 case — ground (b), the Commissioner was misled`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — ground (b), the Commissioner was misled`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (b), the Commissioner was misled`

-- GROUND (c): failure to comply with a required steps notice.
`Article 16 case — ground (c), required steps notice not complied with` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 16(1)(c) — failure to comply with a required steps notice` `Article 16 case — ground (c), required steps notice not complied with`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (c), required steps notice not complied with`

-- GROUND (d): a circumstance PRESCRIBED BY ORDER. The Order's content is unknown to this
-- model — but the ground is LIVE, and the model says so. No silent gap.
`Article 16 case — ground (d), a circumstance prescribed by Order` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 16(1)(d) — a circumstance prescribed by Order applies` `Article 16 case — ground (d), a circumstance prescribed by Order`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — ground (d), a circumstance prescribed by Order`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (d), a circumstance prescribed by Order`

§§ `Article 16(1) — the two hurdles that stop the power arising even where a ground IS made out`

-- THE PROCEDURAL PRECONDITION NOT MET. The charity plainly no longer meets the charity test.
-- The Commissioner thinks deregistration proportionate. But no notice was given, and no
-- objections invited. The DISCRETION DOES NOT ARISE. There is no power to exercise.
`Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `the Quango, no longer meeting the charity test`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS FALSE   -- the failure
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS FALSE   -- and so no invitation either
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the proportionality requirement` `Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the procedural precondition (notice and invitation to object)` `Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given`

-- HALF the procedure: notice given, but it did NOT invite objections within a reasonable
-- time. Both acts are required; one is not enough. The audi alteram partem limb is what is
-- missing, and it is the more important half.
`Article 16 case — notice given, but NO INVITATION to object` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `the Quango, no longer meeting the charity test`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS FALSE   -- the failure
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the procedural precondition (notice and invitation to object)` `Article 16 case — notice given, but NO INVITATION to object`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — notice given, but NO INVITATION to object`

-- PROPORTIONALITY FAILS. Every procedural box ticked; the ground (a technical failure to
-- comply with a required steps notice) is squarely made out; and yet deregistration — the
-- regulatory death penalty — would be disproportionate. The discretion does not arise. This
-- is the limb a regulator is most tempted to skip, and the model will not let it.
`Article 16 case — ground made out, but DISPROPORTIONATE` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS FALSE   -- the failure
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — ground made out, but DISPROPORTIONATE`
#ASSERT `Article 16(1) — the procedural precondition (notice and invitation to object)` `Article 16 case — ground made out, but DISPROPORTIONATE`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the proportionality requirement` `Article 16 case — ground made out, but DISPROPORTIONATE`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — ground made out, but DISPROPORTIONATE`

-- NO GROUND AT ALL: perfect procedure, an entirely proportionate view, and nothing to hang
-- it on. The discretion does not arise.
`Article 16 case — impeccable procedure, but NO GROUND` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS TRUE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS TRUE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — a ground for deregistration is made out` `Article 16 case — impeccable procedure, but NO GROUND`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — impeccable procedure, but NO GROUND`

§§ `Article 16(2) — the MUST`

-- The entity no longer exists. NO notice-and-objection precondition (nobody left to object),
-- NO proportionality gate, NO ground to establish beyond the fact of non-existence. And the
-- Commissioner MUST deregister. Note that on these facts the Art 16(1) DISCRETION does NOT
-- arise at all — no notice, no proportionality finding, no (a)-(d) ground — and yet the
-- Art 16(2) DUTY does. The two routes are independent.
`Article 16 case — the entity no longer exists` MEANS Article16Case WITH
    `the charity`                                                   IS `Jersey Heritage Trust, in good standing`
    `the Commissioner has given the entity written notice specifying reasons`  IS FALSE
    `that notice invited objections within a reasonable time specified in the notice`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that it is proportionate to deregister the entity`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(b) the Commissioner was misled into registering the entity, whether by the entity or by any other person and whether intentionally or otherwise`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(c) the entity has failed to comply with a required steps notice`  IS FALSE
    `(1)(d) a circumstance prescribed by the Minister by Order applies`  IS FALSE
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the entity no longer exists`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT `Article 16(2) — the duty to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — the entity no longer exists`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 16(1) — the discretion to deregister arises` `Article 16 case — the entity no longer exists`

§§ `THE MAY AND THE MUST, TRACED SIDE BY SIDE`

-- (1) The Commissioner EXERCISES the Article 16(1) discretion: deregisters on day 30.
--     The MAY is discharged. FULFILLED.
#TRACE `Article 16(1) — the Commissioner MAY deregister` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `deregister the entity` AT 30

-- (2) The Commissioner DECLINES to exercise it. The ground is made out, the procedure was
--     impeccable, deregistration would be proportionate — and the Commissioner, having
--     considered it all, does nothing. THIS IS NOT A BREACH. Benign omission under a MAY is
--     FULFILLED. That is the whole meaning of a discretion, and it is why Article 16(1) is
--     a power and Article 16(2) is a duty.
#TRACE `Article 16(1) — the Commissioner MAY deregister` `Article 16 case — ground (a), the charity test no longer met` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 400

-- (3) The Commissioner MUST deregister under 16(2), and does. FULFILLED.
#TRACE `Article 16(2) — the Commissioner MUST deregister` `Article 16 case — the entity no longer exists` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `deregister the entity` AT 30

-- (4) The Commissioner MUST deregister under 16(2), and does NOT. Compare trace (2), where
--     the identical inaction was FULFILLED. Here it is a BREACH. Same actor, same inaction,
--     same act left undone — different modality, opposite result.
#TRACE `Article 16(2) — the Commissioner MUST deregister` `Article 16 case — the entity no longer exists` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 400

-- (5) Where the discretion never arose (no notice given), the residual is FULFILLED and the
--     Commissioner has no power to deregister. A deregistration on these facts is not the
--     exercise of a power; it is an act without authority, and Article 34 is the remedy.
#TRACE `Article 16(1) — the Commissioner MAY deregister` `Article 16 case — ground (a) made out, but NO NOTICE was given` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 400

-- (6) The procedural precondition as a LIVE SEQUENCE: the Commissioner gives notice on day
--     7; the entity objects on day 20, inside the 28-day "reasonable time" placeholder.
#TRACE `Article 16(1) — the notice, and the entity's window to object` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `give the entity written notice specifying reasons and inviting objections` AT 7
    PARTY Charity DOES `object to the proposed deregistration` AT 20

-- (7) The entity, having received notice, does not object. Both limbs are MAYs: the entity
--     is not obliged to object, and its silence is not a breach.
#TRACE `Article 16(1) — the notice, and the entity's window to object` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `give the entity written notice specifying reasons and inviting objections` AT 7

§ `Article 17 — Effects of deregistration`

§§ `Article 17(2) and (3) — retrospective deregistration, and the four-limb conjunction`

-- All four limbs made out. Retrospective effect follows, "for all purposes, including the
-- purposes of any enactment relating to taxation".
`the retrospective deregistration` MEANS Article17Deregistration WITH
    name                                                            IS "Jersey Heritage Trust, deregistered retrospectively"
    `the entity`                                                    IS `Jersey Heritage Ltd`
    `(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively`  IS TRUE
    `there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the 'remaining property')`  IS TRUE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes`  IS TRUE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to empower the entity to amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement`  IS TRUE   -- 17(7): irrelevant, and shown to be
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by the Attorney General`  IS TRUE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by a governor of the entity`  IS FALSE
    `(9)(a) it appears to the court that there is or has been any conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property, that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered`  IS TRUE
    `(9)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes of the entity and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor's intention to apply under paragraph (9) and of the reasons for that intention`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 17(2) — the retrospectivity conditions` `the retrospective deregistration`
#ASSERT `Article 17(3) — the termination takes effect from the past date, for all purposes` `the retrospective deregistration`
#ASSERT `Article 17(3) — the entity is treated as unregistered from the past date for the purposes of any enactment relating to taxation` `the retrospective deregistration`

-- LIMB (c) MISSING. The Commissioner is satisfied the grounds have existed since a past
-- date, and determines that deregistration should be retrospective — but never SPECIFIES
-- THE DATE IN THE NOTICE. Paragraph (3) does not apply. There is no retrospective effect,
-- and in particular no retrospective tax consequence. Notice is not decoration.
`the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice` MEANS Article17Deregistration WITH
    name                                                            IS "Jersey Heritage Trust, deregistered without a specified date"
    `the entity`                                                    IS `Jersey Heritage Ltd`
    `(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS FALSE   -- the missing limb
    `(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively`  IS TRUE
    `there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the 'remaining property')`  IS TRUE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes`  IS TRUE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to empower the entity to amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by the Attorney General`  IS FALSE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by a governor of the entity`  IS FALSE
    `(9)(a) it appears to the court that there is or has been any conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property, that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered`  IS FALSE
    `(9)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes of the entity and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor's intention to apply under paragraph (9) and of the reasons for that intention`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(2) — the retrospectivity conditions` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(3) — the termination takes effect from the past date, for all purposes` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(3) — the entity is treated as unregistered from the past date for the purposes of any enactment relating to taxation` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice`

§§ `Article 17(4) — THE CRIMINAL CARVE-OUT, asserted explicitly`

-- ACT 1. The charity, while it believed itself registered, held itself out as a charity and
-- solicited donations on that footing — perfectly lawful at the time. The retrospective
-- deregistration means it was NOT a registered charity when it did so, and Article 21 makes
-- it an offence for an unregistered entity to refer to itself as a charity. Paragraph (3),
-- left to itself, would render this act an offence. THE CARVE-OUT SAVES IT.
`holding itself out as a charity, before the notice` MEANS Article17ActInQuestion WITH
    description                                                     IS "referring to itself as a registered charity while soliciting donations, in the months before the notice"
    `the act occurred before the date of the giving of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS TRUE
    `the act would be rendered an offence by the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS TRUE
    `the act is an offence on a ground independent of the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — the carve-out applies to the act` `the retrospective deregistration` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(4) — the act is an offence` `the retrospective deregistration` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — SAFETY: no pre-notice act is retrospectively criminalised` `the retrospective deregistration` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`

-- ACT 2. The word "MERELY" is load-bearing. The treasurer stole from the collection plate
-- before the notice. That was theft when he did it, and it is theft still: the act is an
-- offence on a ground that owes NOTHING to the retrospective operation of paragraph (3).
-- Article 17(4) is a shield against retrospective criminalisation, not an amnesty.
`theft from the charity, before the notice` MEANS Article17ActInQuestion WITH
    description                                                     IS "the treasurer's theft from the charity's funds, in the months before the notice"
    `the act occurred before the date of the giving of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS TRUE
    `the act would be rendered an offence by the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS FALSE
    `the act is an offence on a ground independent of the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(4) — the carve-out applies to the act` `the retrospective deregistration` `theft from the charity, before the notice`
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — the act is an offence` `the retrospective deregistration` `theft from the charity, before the notice`
-- And the SAFETY PROPERTY still holds — because its antecedent requires that the act NOT be
-- an offence on any independent ground. The property is not violated by a genuine thief.
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — SAFETY: no pre-notice act is retrospectively criminalised` `the retrospective deregistration` `theft from the charity, before the notice`

-- ACT 3. The hard case for the carve-out: an act that is BOTH caught by paragraph (3) AND an
-- offence independently. "Merely" is not satisfied — the independent ground stands on its own
-- feet — so the act IS an offence, and the carve-out does not apply to it.
`fraudulent claim of charitable tax relief, before the notice` MEANS Article17ActInQuestion WITH
    description                                                     IS "dishonestly claiming charitable tax relief, in the months before the notice"
    `the act occurred before the date of the giving of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS TRUE
    `the act would be rendered an offence by the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS TRUE
    `the act is an offence on a ground independent of the operation of paragraph (3)`  IS TRUE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(4) — the carve-out applies to the act` `the retrospective deregistration` `fraudulent claim of charitable tax relief, before the notice`
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — the act is an offence` `the retrospective deregistration` `fraudulent claim of charitable tax relief, before the notice`
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — SAFETY: no pre-notice act is retrospectively criminalised` `the retrospective deregistration` `fraudulent claim of charitable tax relief, before the notice`

-- CONTROL. The same act as ACT 1, on a deregistration that is NOT retrospective (limb (c)
-- was never satisfied). Paragraph (3) is not in operation at all, so there is nothing for
-- the carve-out to carve out of — and nothing that would make the act an offence either.
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(4) — the carve-out applies to the act` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(4) — the act is an offence` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`
#ASSERT `Article 17(4) — SAFETY: no pre-notice act is retrospectively criminalised` `the deregistration where the date was never specified in the notice` `holding itself out as a charity, before the notice`

§§ `Article 17(5) to (8) — the remaining property`

#ASSERT `Article 17(5) — the remaining-property regime applies` `the retrospective deregistration`
#ASSERT `Article 17(6) — the entity applies the remaining property for the preserved purposes` `the retrospective deregistration`
-- ART 17(7), OPERATIVE: the entity's constitution EXPRESSLY purports to empower it to amend
-- the preserved purposes (see the record above) — and it has no such power. "DESPITE any
-- provision of the entity's constitution." The flag appears nowhere in the predicate; that
-- is the point.
#ASSERT `Article 17(7) — the entity has NO power to amend the preserved purposes or statement` `the retrospective deregistration`

-- A deregistered entity with NO remaining property. Paragraphs (6) to (11) do not apply at
-- all: no preserved purposes to serve, no property to protect, no continuing court power.
`the deregistered entity with nothing left` MEANS Article17Deregistration WITH
    name                                                            IS "an entity wound up, with nothing left"
    `the entity`                                                    IS `Jersey Heritage Ltd`
    `(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively`  IS FALSE
    `there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the 'remaining property')`  IS FALSE   -- the gate is shut
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes`  IS FALSE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to empower the entity to amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by the Attorney General`  IS TRUE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by a governor of the entity`  IS FALSE
    `(9)(a) it appears to the court that there is or has been any conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property, that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered`  IS TRUE
    `(9)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes of the entity and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor's intention to apply under paragraph (9) and of the reasons for that intention`  IS FALSE

#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(5) — the remaining-property regime applies` `the deregistered entity with nothing left`
-- 17(6) is satisfied VACUOUSLY: there is no remaining property to misapply.
#ASSERT `Article 17(6) — the entity applies the remaining property for the preserved purposes` `the deregistered entity with nothing left`
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(7) — the entity has NO power to amend the preserved purposes or statement` `the deregistered entity with nothing left`
-- And the court's Article 17(9) power does NOT arise, even though everything else about the
-- application is in order: the gate in 17(5) is shut.
#ASSERT NOT `Article 17(9) — the court's power over the deregistered entity arises` `the deregistered entity with nothing left`

§§ `Article 17(9) and 17(10) — the court's continuing powers`

-- The Attorney General applies, on ground (9)(a). The power arises. 17(10) is vacuous: the
-- applicant is not a governor.
#ASSERT `Article 17(10) — a governor's application is competent` `the retrospective deregistration`
#ASSERT `Article 17(9) — the court's power over the deregistered entity arises` `the retrospective deregistration`

-- A GOVERNOR applies — WITHOUT first giving notice to the Attorney General. Article 17(10)
-- is a condition precedent: the application is INCOMPETENT, and the court's power does not
-- arise, however meritorious the substance.
`a governor's application, without notice to the Attorney General` MEANS Article17Deregistration WITH
    name                                                            IS "a governor's application, made without notice to the AG"
    `the entity`                                                    IS `Jersey Heritage Ltd`
    `(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16`  IS TRUE
