    `the entity`                                                    IS `Jersey Heritage Ltd`
    `(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16`  IS TRUE
    `(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)`  IS FALSE
    `(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively`  IS FALSE
    `there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the 'remaining property')`  IS TRUE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes`  IS FALSE
    `the entity continues to apply the remaining property in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `a provision of the entity's constitution or constitutional Law purports to empower the entity to amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement`  IS FALSE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by the Attorney General`  IS FALSE
    `the application under paragraph (9) is made by a governor of the entity`  IS TRUE
    `(9)(a) it appears to the court that there is or has been any conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property, that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered`  IS TRUE
    `(9)(b) it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes of the entity and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement`  IS TRUE
    `the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor's intention to apply under paragraph (9) and of the reasons for that intention`  IS TRUE   -- the only change

#ASSERT `Article 17(10) — a governor's application is competent` `a governor's application, with notice to the Attorney General`
#ASSERT `Article 17(9) — the court's power over the deregistered entity arises` `a governor's application, with notice to the Attorney General`

§§ `Article 17 — the duties and powers, traced`

-- Art 17(1): the Commissioner retains the record for 10 years and never reassigns the
-- number. Both limbs honoured: the MUST performed, the SHANT refrained from.
#TRACE `Article 17(1) — retention, and the registered number` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `retain the information registered in respect of the entity for 10 years` AT 100

-- Art 17(1): the Commissioner reassigns the registered number to a new charity. SHANT —
-- doing the act IS the breach, whatever else was done right.
#TRACE `Article 17(1) — retention, and the registered number` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `retain the information registered in respect of the entity for 10 years` AT 100
    PARTY Commissioner DOES `assign the registered number of that entity to another registered charity` AT 500

-- Art 17(6): the deregistered entity keeps applying the remaining property for the preserved
-- charitable purposes.
#TRACE `Article 17(6) — the duty to apply the remaining property for the preserved purposes` `the retrospective deregistration` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes` AT 90

-- Art 17(7): the deregistered entity purports to amend the preserved charitable purposes by
-- its own act, relying on its constitution. It has NO power to do so, and the SHANT makes
-- the attempt a breach.
#TRACE `Article 17(7) — the entity SHANT amend the preserved purposes or statement` `the retrospective deregistration` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement` AT 90

-- Art 17(8): the LAWFUL route to the same end. The entity applies to the court; the court
-- makes the amending order. Self-help is a breach; an application is a permission.
#TRACE `Article 17(8) — application to the court to amend the preserved purposes` `the retrospective deregistration` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement` AT 90
    PARTY Court DOES `make such amending order, and any ancillary order, as the court considers expedient` AT 150

-- Art 17(9): the court exercises its continuing Article 14 power over the deregistered entity.
#TRACE `Article 17(9) — the court's continuing power to make an order` `the retrospective deregistration` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Court DOES `make an order in respect of the charity` AT 60

-- Art 17(9), where the power did not arise (no remaining property): FULFILLED, no power.
#TRACE `Article 17(9) — the court's continuing power to make an order` `the deregistered entity with nothing left` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Charity DOES `send an annual return` AT 300

-- Art 17(10)-(11): the governor gives the Attorney General notice; the Attorney General takes
-- the application over and proceeds with it.
#TRACE `Articles 17(10) and 17(11) — the governor's notice and the Attorney General's takeover` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Governor DOES `give notice to the Attorney General of the intention to apply and of the reasons` AT 10
    PARTY AttorneyGeneral DOES `take over the application made by the governor` AT 25
    PARTY AttorneyGeneral DOES `proceed with the application` AT 60

-- The same, but the Attorney General takes it over and WITHDRAWS it. The governor's
-- application dies in the Attorney General's hands: "may then withdraw or proceed with the
-- application as the Attorney General sees fit."
#TRACE `Articles 17(10) and 17(11) — the governor's notice and the Attorney General's takeover` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Governor DOES `give notice to the Attorney General of the intention to apply and of the reasons` AT 10
    PARTY AttorneyGeneral DOES `take over the application made by the governor` AT 25
    PARTY AttorneyGeneral DOES `withdraw the application` AT 60

-- The governor gives notice; the Attorney General does NOT take the application over. A MAY:
-- benign omission, FULFILLED. The governor's application proceeds in the governor's own name.
#TRACE `Articles 17(10) and 17(11) — the governor's notice and the Attorney General's takeover` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Governor DOES `give notice to the Attorney General of the intention to apply and of the reasons` AT 10
